# pleamar-wm's session: ~/.config/pleamar/session.conf (`pleamar-wm init` puts
# this there).
# What is not said here is taken from Hyprland's configuration (~/.config/hypr:
# its monitor lines, kb_layout, accel_profile, sensitivity), if there is one.
# `pleamar-wm config` shows what comes out.

# monitor NAME  WxH@HZ | preferred | highest  [at X,Y]  [scale S]  [transform 90|180|270]  [vrr]  [off]
#monitor DP-3 1920x1080@165 at 0,0
#monitor HDMI-A-1 preferred at 1920,0
# A monitor standing on its side: `transform 90` (or 270, the other way round),
# the same numbers as Hyprland's `transform` (1 = 90, 3 = 270), which is read too.
#monitor DP-2 preferred at 1920,0 transform 90

# The programs pinned to the shore (the dock of a free monitor), in order: by
# their .desktop's name (org.telegram.desktop, or its last part, telegram),
# the class their windows say, or their name.
#dock kitty zen-browser org.telegram.desktop

# How the shore behaves (the control centre's Dock page writes it): hidden
# until the mouse touches its edge, the icons swelling under the mouse, a drop
# under each program per window, windows put away into their program's icon,
# the icons' size (32 to 72), and its edge: bottom, left or right.
#dock-style hide off magnify off dots on into off size 44 side bottom

# The blur behind a panel, a bar or a menu that asks for it (dual Kawase):
# passes 1-6 (how wide), offset 0.5-8 (how far each pass reaches), noise
# 0-0.5 (a grain, so it does not band), saturation 0-3 and brightness 0-3
# (1, as it is behind); menus on also blurs behind every window's menus,
# not only the ones that ask for it, but not where they are less opaque than
# menus-ignorealpha 0-1 (their rounded corners and shadows; 0, everywhere).
#blur passes 3 offset 1 noise 0 saturation 1 brightness 1 menus off menus-ignorealpha 0.2

# How a monitor starts: one of leader, columns, rows, grid, leader-right,
# scrolling (as niri), marea (the one used most is widest), fractal (split and
# split again, and zoom), or free (each window where it is left). `*` for every
# monitor; the last line that names one wins. pleamar-wm's control centre
# (`pleamar-wm control`) writes these.
#layout * leader
#layout HDMI-A-1 scrolling

# What some windows do when they open: by their program (`app=`, its app id or
# X11 class) and/or their title (`title=`), no case, `*` for anything.
#   window app=NAME|title=TEXT [float] [size WxH] [monitor N|NAME] [workspace N] [private] [frame=own|ours]
#   (private: pixelated in whatever is shared of a whole screen, and in its screenshots)
#   (frame=own: the program draws its own title bar and edges; frame=ours: the
#   session draws them, for a program that lets it. GTK 4 programs always draw
#   their own, and are carried and stretched from it all the same)
#window app=pavucontrol float size 820x560
#window app=discord workspace 3 monitor HDMI-A-1
#window title="Picture in Picture" float
#window app=org.keepassxc.KeePassXC private
#window app=zen frame=own

# keyboard  layout L  variant V  options O  repeat KEYS_A_SECOND  delay MS
#keyboard layout es repeat 25 delay 400

# pointer / touchpad  accel flat|adaptive  speed -1..1  natural on|off  tap on|off  dwt on|off
#pointer accel flat
#touchpad tap on natural on

# idle  off-after SECONDS  (the monitors go dark; any input wakes them)
#idle off-after 600

# phone lock COMMAND  what locks the session when it comes back from the phone
# to the desk: `marea lock` if unsaid; `none`, nothing (docs/phone.md)
#phone lock marea lock

# agent on|off  a computer-use agent (Cua Driver) may use the windows with a
# pointer and a keyboard of its own: your mouse and your focus stay yours, and
# its cursors are drawn moving. Off unless said: what has it can type anywhere.
#agent on
